## Purpose

Stacked Core-v1 authorization work on top of PR #165.

PR #165 defines and enforces the provider-neutral Core boundary. This PR closes the next audited Core gap: `WorkingState.capability_grant_refs` exist, but the generic Core does not yet have a typed deterministic authorization contract binding one active grant to one exact attempted action/resource/context.

Base branch: `research/cap-core-v1-completion`.

## Stage Research

`project-context/CAP_CORE_V1_AUTHORIZATION_REENTRY.md`

Decision: **NARROW**.

Selected first slice:

- pure provider-neutral `authorization.py`;
- immutable bounded `CapabilityGrant`;
- immutable bounded `AuthorizationRequest`;
- `AuthorizationDecision`;
- deterministic exact-match `authorize_request()`;
- adversarial mismatch/fingerprint tests;
- add authorization to the machine-readable Core-v1 manifest.

No physical behavior changes in this first slice.

## Explicitly not selected

- OPA/Cedar runtime;
- generic policy language;
- RBAC/ABAC platform;
- OAuth/MCP credentials as CAP local grants;
- wildcard filesystem/URL/window policy in Core;
- provider registry or generic dispatcher;
- new public Chat tool.

## Promotion boundary

Core v1 is not frozen until later consumer migrations bind the resulting decision into the mutating path:

```text
active WorkingState grant
 -> exact AuthorizationRequest
 -> AuthorizationDecision
 -> LoopGuard
 -> physical effect
```

Those migrations remain separate, consumer-specific acceptance work.

## Sequencing

This PR is stacked and **must not merge before #165**, and #165 itself remains blocked behind the exact-base completion of #159.

